# {{name}}

{{summary}}

This project was created with `awa init`. The whole process lives in
`blueprint.yaml` (roles, tools, collaboration and approvals); the tools it
calls are plain Python functions in `tools.py`.

| File | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `blueprint.yaml` | The process: agents, instructions, team or workflow, approvals. |
| `tools.py` | The tools the agents call. Replace the sample data with your external systems. |
| `tests/test_blueprint.py` | Offline tests: scripted models, no API key, no network. |
| `evals/cases.yaml` | Eval cases for `awa eval`, run against a real model. |
| `.env.example` | The settings awa reads. `awa setup` writes your `.env`, or copy this file to `.env`. |

## Set up

awa is already installed (you ran `awa init`); this project does not install it again and
asks no package index for it. Work in the same environment (`awa doctor` shows which one).

```bash
pip install pytest      # for the offline tests, if it is missing
pytest                  # offline tests, no key
awa setup               # set your provider's key in .env (or: awa ui, then Set up)
```

## Run

```bash
awa validate blueprint.yaml                         # check the blueprint and its tools
awa show blueprint.yaml --mermaid                   # draw the topology
awa chat blueprint.yaml                             # talk to the system
awa run blueprint.yaml "{{example_request}}" --stream
```

In `awa chat`, approval-gated tool calls are asked for inline; `/help` lists
the commands (`/reset`, `/usage`, `/history`, `/exit`).

## Serve it as an HTTP API

Install the `server` extra into awa once; `awa doctor` prints the exact command for your install.

```bash
awa serve blueprint.yaml
```

## Test and evaluate

```bash
pytest                                              # offline, deterministic
awa eval blueprint.yaml evals/cases.yaml
```

The tests give every agent its own scripted model (`awa.testing.FakeChatModel`),
so they check the wiring of the process (who delegates to whom, which tools
run, which calls wait for approval) without calling a provider. The
evaluation runs the real model on `evals/cases.yaml` and exits with code 1
when a case fails, so it can gate a deployment in CI.

## Next steps

1. Replace the sample data in `tools.py` with calls to your external systems.
2. Adjust the instructions in `blueprint.yaml` and re-run `awa validate`.
3. Add a test per rule that matters and an eval case per typical request.
